---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import DocShell from "../../components/DocShell.astro";
import CommandBlock from "../../components/CommandBlock.astro";
---

<Layout title="Getting Started | ArkheionX Docs" description="Install ArkheionX and produce a first local review map.">
  <DocShell>
    <p class="page-kicker">Start</p>
    <h1>Getting started.</h1>
    <p class="doc-deck">Install from public source, verify the command, and run the first mapping pass against a repository you are authorized to inspect.</p>

    <h2>1. Install</h2>
    <CommandBlock command="curl -fsSL https://arkheionx.dev/install | bash" />
    <p>The installer targets GitHub <code>main</code>. Inspect-first and manual alternatives are documented on the <a href="/install.html">install page</a>.</p>

    <h2>2. Verify the environment</h2>
    <CommandBlock command={`arkheionx version
arkheionx doctor .`} />
    <p><code>doctor</code> checks the Python package, command path, project layout, and Foundry availability. Foundry detection does not run a build.</p>

    <h2>3. Use a controlled demo</h2>
    <CommandBlock command={`arkheionx demo --list
arkheionx demo --copy oracle-staking /tmp/arkheionx-demo
arkheionx review-map /tmp/arkheionx-demo`} />
    <p>Bundled demos are local toy fixtures. They are useful for learning artifact structure without attaching claims to a production protocol.</p>

    <h2>4. Map your repository</h2>
    <CommandBlock command={`cd /path/to/authorized-foundry-project
arkheionx scan .
arkheionx review-map .`} />
    <p>Review generated files under <code>.arkheionx/out/</code>. Do not treat the terminal summary as the complete artifact or as a security conclusion.</p>

    <div class="doc-note"><strong>Boundary:</strong> repository authorization and handling policy are your responsibility. Keep sensitive output local unless the review scope permits sharing.</div>
  </DocShell>
</Layout>
